ITEMS = [
('h2', 'CHAPTER\t7\tCONCLUSION'),
('h3', '7.1  Conclusion'),
('p', "This project started from a simple but under-studied observation: a machine-learning service on Kubernetes can be perfectly healthy by every standard probe and still serve wrong answers. The work re-engineered the Bangalore Climate Analytics application (SARIMA and a real trained LSTM, unchanged) into a containerised, orchestrated and observable system, built a benchmark of silent faults, and designed and measured a layered set of mechanisms that make the platform aware of the correctness of the answers."),
('p', "The measurements support five conclusions. First, default Docker Compose and Kubernetes never detect or recover from silent faults: all seven silent faults produced 100 percent wrong answers with the guard off, and nothing was detected in 10 of 10 trials per fault. Second, a window-level conformal envelope with temporal invariants, a cross-model canary and a golden-output integrity check turned every injected silent fault into a valid, labelled answer (0 percent wrong in all fault types, including subtle bit flips that evade the other layers) with zero false alarms in healthy control, in a ten-minute soak and on hold-out windows. Third, automatic restoration of full quality needs a correctness-aware recovery loop: with the operator the service returned to full quality in 20 of 20 trials in about 22 to 24 seconds, whereas it never returned without it, and a cost model that charges for time below full quality was necessary for the learned policy to behave sensibly. Fourth, a deadline- and load-aware degradation mechanism removed the failures caused by slow model calls (96.8 percent to 0 percent failed requests) and reduced the p95 latency under burst load by about four times. Fifth, the extras show that precursor-based prediction gives a useful warning time for gradual faults (69.6 s for memory leaks), that a shadow gate separates good and bad candidate models cleanly, and that the same criterion can drive a standard delivery tool (Argo Rollouts)."),
('p', "The project also produced negative and inconclusive results that are as important as the positive ones: probe tuning is secondary to redundancy, a learned bandit offers no advantage over simple rules in the available action space, log-based detection has no signal for silent faults, the input guard and the LSTM autoencoder generate too many false alarms, and conclusions about autoscaling on a single node could not be drawn. Recording these results, and withdrawing a claim that a later experiment contradicted, was part of the method."),

('h3', '7.2  Limitations'),
('bullets', [
 "Scale: 5 to 10 trials per experimental cell, one model family, one data set, one laptop with a one-node Minikube cluster; confidence intervals are therefore wide for some cells and multi-node effects are not studied.",
 "The golden-output check detects change from a trusted start-up state; it cannot detect a model that was already wrong when it started, and legitimate retraining requires refreshing the golden outputs after the shadow gate.",
 "Faults are injected synthetically (weights, scaler, output, delay); real-world incidents may differ. The injected faults were designed by the authors, which can favour the proposed detectors.",
 "The cross-model canary and the golden check assume deterministic models; stochastic models need statistical variants.",
 "Most delayed requests end on the climatology tier because all backends share one lock; per-tier locks were not implemented.",
 "The learned recovery policy has only restart and tier-switch actions, so it cannot outperform the rules policy; its benefit would appear with a richer action set.",
 "Scaling results (HPA, pre-scaling, KEDA) were obtained on a single node with an earlier thread configuration and are withdrawn as evidence; the RL autoscaler is a simulation only.",
 "Security: placeholder secrets and unauthenticated fault and shadow endpoints are acceptable only in the lab (see the security review); the GitHub Actions workflow, Trivy scan and keyless signing were written but could not be run on the laptop.",
 "The metamorphic probe is experimental: marginal against subtle bit flips and sensitive to drift when calibrated on earlier years.",
 "Novelty claims are limited to the 23 papers reviewed and a limited web search; a structured search of IEEE Xplore and Google Scholar is still required.",
]),
('h3', '7.3  Future Scope'),
('bullets', [
 "Improve the metamorphic readiness probe (Section 6.6): periodic re-calibration against drift, a larger and more diverse set of probe windows, and relations with more power against subtle weight corruption.",
 "New metrics: healthy-but-wrong time and Mean Time To Correctness (MTTC) as complements to availability and MTTR.",
 "Per-tier locks and finer admission control for deadline-aware degradation; automatic tuning of the deadline from the latency SLO.",
 "A multi-node cluster study of autoscaling with warm-up awareness (HPA, KEDA and a learned policy) using the 16-thread image.",
 "Generalisation to other models and data sets (a second forecasting workload, classification services) and to stochastic models.",
 "A richer action set for the operator (scale up, roll back to the previous model, switch tier permanently) so that the learned policy can show a benefit.",
 "Running the CI pipeline with image signing, Trivy scanning and Kyverno verification on GitHub, adding authentication to the control endpoints, and network policies.",
 "Comparison with a literature baseline for output validation (simple range or threshold detector) and larger numbers of trials for the headline claims.",
 "A structured literature search and a conference or journal paper based on Appendix B.",
]),
('h3', '7.4  Summary'),
('p', "The study demonstrates, with measurements and with an honest account of its limits, that the self-healing promise of container orchestration can be extended from process health to answer correctness at modest cost, and it provides the code, data and documents needed to reproduce and extend the work."),
]

