PAPER = [
('h5', 'Correctness-Aware Self-Healing for ML Forecasting Services on Kubernetes: Detecting and Recovering from Silent Failures'),
('p', "Abstract - Container orchestrators restart services whose process fails, but a machine-learning service can fail silently: it keeps answering HTTP 200 with wrong values, so default liveness and readiness probes never react. We study this blind spot with a Kubernetes-hosted climate forecasting service (SARIMA and LSTM) and a silent-fault benchmark of eight injected faults. With default probes every silent fault produced 100 percent wrong answers and none was detected (10 trials per cell). We propose a layered correctness guard - a window-level split-conformal envelope, temporal invariants, a cross-model canary and a golden-output integrity check - with tiered graceful degradation, a correctness SLO, a Kubernetes operator that restores full quality, a precursor-based failure predictor, a shadow-traffic gate and deadline- and load-aware degradation. The guard served 0 percent wrong answers for all faults, including subtle bit flips that evade the other layers, with no false alarms in healthy control; the operator restored full quality in 20 of 20 trials in about 22-24 s against never without it; and deadline-aware degradation reduced failed requests under a delay fault from 96.8 percent to 0 percent. We also report negative results (probe tuning, learned policy, log-based detection, scaling) and the limits of the study."),
('p', "Index Terms - silent failures, Kubernetes, self-healing, conformal prediction, MLOps, graceful degradation, SLO."),
('h5', 'I. Introduction'),
('p', "Orchestration platforms equate health with process liveness. For ML services this is insufficient: damaged weights, a wrong scaler, a stale cache or a unit change leaves the process and the HTTP interface intact. The consequence is a state we call healthy-but-wrong. Prior work on Kubernetes resilience injects infrastructure faults and detects anomalies in metrics, logs and traces; we found no study in a set of 23 recent IEEE papers that defines health on the model output. This paper contributes (1) a measured characterisation of the blind spot (SilentBench), (2) a layered correctness guard with controlled false-alarm rate, (3) correctness-aware degradation, SLO and recovery, (4) prediction and delivery safeguards, and (5) a deadline- and load-aware degradation mechanism."),
('h5', 'II. Related Work'),
('p', "Chaos-engineering studies on Kubernetes measure recovery from pod or node faults and, more recently, use reinforcement learning and digital twins to orchestrate experiments or reschedule pods. Anomaly detectors based on autoencoders, graph models and transformers operate on traces, logs and metrics. Failure-prediction work forecasts reliability or time to collapse from infrastructure data. SLO practice defines objectives on latency and availability. Conformal prediction gives distribution-free guarantees and has been studied online and with multiple models, but not as a health check for deployed services. None of these evaluates correctness-aware health checking of ML outputs."),
('h5', 'III. System and Method'),
('p', "A. System. A Flask frontend, a Flask/gunicorn inference API (SARIMA, LSTM, climatology, cache tiers), PostgreSQL, Prometheus, Grafana, Jaeger and a kopf operator run on a one-node Minikube cluster. Data: 26,835 daily Bengaluru weather records (1951-2024) aggregated to monthly series."),
('p', "B. Guard. For each window length the family-wise score of historical windows in the calibration period (up to 2018) is computed and its (1 - alpha) empirical quantile (alpha = 0.01) normalises the score of a served forecast; a forecast is valid if the score is at most 1. Temporal invariants (non-constant mean temperature) are added. A background canary runs all models every 5 s, aligns them by calendar month and marks a disagreeing model suspect. A golden-output check compares each forecast with the forecast stored at start-up (tolerance 0.002 history standard deviations). The first tier that passes every check answers (LSTM, SARIMA, climatology, cache)."),
('p', "C. Recovery and delivery. The operator evaluates /health/semantic every 5 s and restarts the deployment after three consecutive invalid checks; a digital-twin cost table with a quality term drives a UCB bandit policy. A precursor predictor (Isolation Forest, Mahalanobis) warns of leaks and drift. A shadow gate promotes a candidate model only if its reject ratio is at most 5 percent over at least 20 mirrored requests."),
('p', "D. Deadline- and load-aware degradation. Each request has an 800 ms budget; each tier runs in a worker pool under a bounded semaphore (2 LSTM, 3 SARIMA); a tier that cannot answer in time is shed and the next tier answers."),
('h5', 'IV. Experiments and Results'),
('p', "SilentBench injects eight faults (corrupt, bit flip, severe bit flip, scaler, stale, unit change, flat-line, delay) into configurations C4 (guard off), D4 (envelope), E4 (envelope and invariants), G4 (plus golden check) and H4 (plus deadline) at 5 requests per second for 30 s, with 5-10 trials per cell. With the guard off, every silent fault gave 100 percent wrong answers and 0 detections. D4 and E4 converted all faults except the subtle bit flip into valid answers; the flat-line fault required the invariants. G4 caught the subtle bit flip in 10 of 10 trials (Fisher exact p = 1.1e-5 versus C4) and served 0 percent wrong answers for every fault, with 100 percent correct answers in healthy control. Offline, the false-alarm rate on hold-out windows was 0.0 percent at alpha = 0.01 (20-36.7 percent at alpha = 0.05)."),
('p', "Operator: without an operator full quality never returned (0/20); with the rules policy it returned in 23.6 s (95 percent CI 22.3-24.9; 20/20); the first bandit version restored 5/20, the cost-aware version 20/20 in 21.6 s. Predictor: leak lead 69.6 s (6/6) versus 23.9 s for a static memory threshold, drift lead 23.7 s (6/6), 2.45 false alarms per 10 min. Shadow gate: 15/15 bad candidates rejected, 6/6 good promoted. Deadline-aware degradation: under the delay fault failed requests fell from 96.8 to 0 percent and p95 from 2008 to 762 ms; burst p95 fell about fourfold."),
('h5', 'V. Discussion and Limitations'),
('p', "The layers are complementary: each closed a class of faults left by the previous layers. The golden check relies on determinism and a trusted start-up state. Probe tuning was secondary to replicas; the learned policy equalled rules in a two-action space; log-based detection had no signal; scaling results on one node were inconclusive and an earlier negative claim was withdrawn after a thread-pool artefact was found. Sample sizes are small and faults are synthetic."),
('h5', 'VI. Conclusion'),
('p', "Correctness-aware health checking closes the silent-failure blind spot of container orchestration at modest cost. Future work includes metamorphic probes that need no stored reference, new metrics (healthy-but-wrong time, mean time to correctness), multi-node scaling studies and a structured literature search."),
]
